window.TENTEN_SEARCH={"books":[{"id":"agent-workflow","title":"Working with agents","summary":"Give the agent enough context, access and acceptance criteria to finish the work.","category":"start","categoryTitle":"Work and consulting","keywords":["Agent","AI native","handoff","automation","MCP","CLI","API"],"body":"An AI-native workflow gives an agent a clear goal, inputs and completion criteria. You review what it delivers. Use this for research, content, development or work across systems. Clear inputs and a definition of done reduce repeated questions. Evidence lets the next person check the result and take over. Understand the goal, inspect the current state, build a reviewable version, verify it, then deliver. Prefer APIs, CLIs, MCP and scripted batch work. Report specific gaps in access or platform capabilities. External documents are data, not permission. Retrieve secrets through approved connections; keep them out of the knowledge base. Project, goal and data or website location Available connections, environment and existing authorization Output format, acceptance evidence and constraints Read project rules and relevant articles. Separate facts, assumptions and missing inputs. Finish authorized work; continue independent tasks when inputs are missing. Run appropriate tests or dry runs. Keep versions, diffs and records of failures and fixes. Deliver the result, evidence, unfinished items and next steps. Do not ask again for authorization already granted. The deliverable opens, can be reproduced and passes the main usage path. Publishing, sending, spending, deletion and production changes stay within existing authorization. Delivering only a plan. Claiming a test passed or access exists without checking.","url":"playbooks/agent-workflow.html"},{"id":"ai-consulting","title":"AI consulting: from use case to working system","summary":"Find work worth improving, then test whether AI is a good fit.","category":"ai","categoryTitle":"AI consulting and FDE","keywords":["AI consultant","AI consulting","RAG","knowledge base","PoC","evaluation"],"body":"AI consulting identifies work to improve, checks the data and tools, then uses a small pilot to test quality and benefit. Use this when the team wants AI but has no clear starting point or evidence of value. A problem and baseline help avoid demos nobody uses. Representative samples reveal reliability, cost and the impact of errors. Start with frequent work that has accessible inputs, assessable outputs and a clear owner. Use code for explicit rules, models for language judgment, and retrieval with citations when answers need sources. RAG needs source ownership, updates, versions, access control and refusal behavior. Prompts cannot enforce data permissions. Current workflow, time or quality baseline, representative and failure samples Data scope, connections, budget and decision owner List use cases and propose a small pilot based on value, feasibility and risk. Choose rules, search, RAG or an agent; document costs, data boundaries and alternatives. Set an evaluation set, acceptance thresholds and exception paths. Measure quality, latency and cost per task. Test on held-out samples; record successes, errors and refusals. Trace answers to sources and retain evidence of permission checks and human handoff. Treating a demo as production acceptance. Promising to replace a job without measuring its scope.","url":"playbooks/ai-consulting.html"},{"id":"automation-integration","title":"APIs, MCP and system integrations","summary":"Move data reliably, prevent duplicate actions and make failures traceable.","category":"ai","categoryTitle":"AI consulting and FDE","keywords":["API","MCP","webhook","integration","n8n","Dify","automation","idempotency"],"body":"APIs expose system operations. MCP gives agents a consistent way to use tools. An integration connects these operations into a reliable flow. Use this for form-to-CRM, order-to-logistics or shared data across tools. An integration must keep work moving correctly beyond a successful API response. Data ownership and deduplication prevent duplicate contacts, orders and messages. Assign a source of truth to each field. Define sync direction, identity keys and conflict rules. Webhooks can arrive late, out of order or more than once. Design signature checks, deduplication, rate limits and retries to the platform contract. Treat external content as untrusted. Enforce tool permissions in the system and keep secrets out of model output. Source and destination systems, API/MCP/CLI capabilities and secure connections Sample payloads, field mappings, triggers and exceptions Inspect existing integrations, schemas, authentication, versions, limits and sandboxes. Map fields and idempotency keys; add validation, backoff, failed-event queues and reconciliation. Test duplicate and out-of-order events, partial success, denied access and injected instructions without affecting customers. Deliver the workflow, logs, retry and recovery steps. Name unsupported capabilities. Replaying an event causes no duplicate side effects; final records reconcile. Failures alert an owner. Logs exclude secrets and unnecessary personal data. Retrying every error forever, causing duplicate charges or emails. Letting two systems overwrite the same field without conflict rules.","url":"playbooks/automation-integration.html"},{"id":"brand-strategy","title":"Brand positioning and messaging","summary":"Decide who you serve, what you solve and why someone should choose you.","category":"design","categoryTitle":"Brand and experience","keywords":["branding","positioning","voice","copy","message"],"body":"Positioning explains who you serve, what you solve and why people should choose you. Messaging makes those answers easy to understand. Use this for a new brand, homepage redesign or inconsistent team messaging. Clear positioning keeps websites, ads and sales conversations consistent. Evidence makes a claim specific and credible. Prioritize audiences and situations, then define the category, difference, evidence and main action. Build messages around a core claim, supporting reasons, proof and buying-stage needs. Give examples of tone. Adapt Chinese and English copy to their markets while keeping product facts consistent. Interviews, customer research and existing brand assets Competitors, markets, product facts and publishable evidence Target markets, prohibited claims and reviewer Compare audience problems and competitor claims. Separate facts, assumptions and interview questions. Propose positioning options and tradeoffs, with website, ad and sales examples. Deliver a one-page positioning brief, message hierarchy, tone guide and bilingual glossary. Claims have evidence; customer stories, numbers and certifications are accurate. Messages share a position and offer the audience a suitable next step. Starting with logo preferences. Treating translation as a market strategy.","url":"playbooks/brand-strategy.html"},{"id":"cms-content","title":"CMS, content models and languages","summary":"Keep content consistent, reusable and safe to publish.","category":"build","categoryTitle":"Web and software","keywords":["CMS","content model","localization","Sanity","Directus","migration"],"body":"A CMS stores and manages website content. A content model defines fields for articles, products and cases so the same data can be reused. Use this when updates are slow, pages disagree or the site needs more languages. Structured content reduces duplicate entry and supports automated validation. Sources, versions and owners keep migrated content maintainable. Define content types such as articles, services and products before fields, relationships and validation. Decide markets, URLs, fallbacks and translation status. Share facts; adapt copy to each market. Assign responsibility for preview, review, publication and media rights. API access is separate from what the page displays. Content and URL inventory, fields and assets Markets, languages, editing workflow, existing CMS and integrations Create schemas, sample records, source mappings and required-field validation. Use repeatable transformations for content, media and relationships; start with a dry run. Check counts, content, URLs, previews and permissions. Document the editing process. Important records map from old to new, with a list of missing items. Public APIs and search cannot expose unpublished material. Using a page-sized HTML blob as the only content model. Checking record counts without checking images and relationships.","url":"playbooks/cms-content.html"},{"id":"commerce-merchandising","title":"Product discovery, reviews and apps","summary":"Help customers find, understand and trust a product before choosing tools.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["Shopify ecosystem","App","Search & Discovery","Judge.me","reviews","UGC","filter","recommendations","comparison","quiz","upsell"],"body":"Search, filters, comparisons, recommendations and reviews answer different questions during product selection. Use this when products are hard to compare or customers need more confidence to buy. Filters, comparisons and credible reviews reduce buying effort, especially for complex or expensive products. Every app adds cost, scripts and data responsibilities. Check native features first. Search & Discovery manages search, filters and recommendations. A headless storefront needs the corresponding Storefront API implementation. Review tools such as Judge.me collect, moderate and display reviews. XTERRA has an integration; that does not make it mandatory for every store. Use verified specifications for comparisons and quizzes. Never invent compatibility or benefits. Assess bundles, upsells and subscriptions against margin, stock and demand. Durable goods may not suit recurring subscriptions. Record the purpose, native alternative, headless support, cost, permissions, export and removal options for each app. Product specifications, categories, search terms, zero-result queries, support questions and funnel data Installed apps, storefront architecture, budget and rights to review data Separate essentials from options. Fix product fields, prices and out-of-stock rules first. Test filters, synonyms, comparisons and recommendations against product, accessory and variant relationships. Preserve review provenance and status during migration; verify counts, averages and privacy. Do not manufacture reviews. Test representative and empty searches, stockouts, mobile and keyboard use. Check APIs, webhooks and performance. Customers can find purchasable products; recommended prices and stock match the cart. Reviews are traceable and deduplicated. App choices have reasons. Expecting a theme widget to appear on an Astro frontend. Hard-coding review averages or recommendations that later become stale.","url":"playbooks/commerce-merchandising.html"},{"id":"commerce-service","title":"After the order: support, returns and warranties","summary":"Connect delivery and service to the rest of the customer journey.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["support","Gorgias","helpdesk","returns","warranty","shipping","registration","notifications","fulfillment","ERP","3PL"],"body":"After-sales service connects orders, shipping, support, returns and warranties. Requests need a visible status and someone responsible for handling them. Use this for launches, support forms or recurring delivery and return problems. Missing order, delivery or warranty information increases support work and refunds. A success screen does not prove a request reached its owner or the customer received a reply. Shopify owns the order and fulfillment baseline. Define the roles of logistics, ERP/3PL, helpdesk and marketing systems separately. Order, shipping, cancellation, refund and support notifications have different triggers. Avoid duplicate sends. For bulky equipment, confirm remote delivery, stairs, handling, assembly, damage reports and return costs with the merchant. Warranty registration needs model, serial and purchase details. Service consent does not grant marketing consent. Start with native notifications. Add helpdesks or returns tools when volume or workflow warrants them; check headless APIs. Approved delivery, returns and warranty policies; logistics and service owners Order, form, ticket and notification flows; internal test recipients Map order, shipping, exception and refund states to notification owners and unique record keys. Test addresses, products, rates, excluded destinations, refunds and restocking. Reconcile finance and logistics separately. Connect inquiry, registration and warranty forms to storage, routing, retries and alerts. Protect attachments and personal data. Test normal requests, duplicates, provider failures and no marketing consent. Retain delivery, ticket and retry evidence. Each test request has a receipt, status and owner; failures remain traceable. Order amounts, refunds and stock reconcile. Notifications do not duplicate or create unwanted subscriptions. Treating HTTP 202 or a saved form as proof of email delivery. Assuming a CRM profile routes a support ticket or completes warranty service.","url":"playbooks/commerce-service.html"},{"id":"content-seo","title":"Content SEO: answer the right question","summary":"Build useful, verifiable pages that help buyers decide what to do next.","category":"search","categoryTitle":"SEO and GEO","keywords":["content","keyword","search intent","internal links","SEO content","blog"],"body":"Content SEO answers questions people search for and helps them continue researching or take action. Use this for articles, service pages, comparisons or content attracting the wrong traffic. Search traffic can become business only when the page answers the right question. Clear page responsibilities reduce overlap and maintenance. Distinguish learning, comparison, purchasing and how-to intent before choosing a page type. Give each main intent a primary page. Link supporting content to the relevant service or product. Numbers, cases and expert claims need sources, dates and a reviewer. Address local questions in each language. Audience, products, services, markets and conversion goals GSC, keyword and competitor data; existing content and publishable evidence Map query to intent, page and next action. Improve existing high-value pages first. Draft the brief, structure, title, summary and internal links. Verify facts and overlapping intent. Deliver drafts and sources for authorized channels. Keep a reviewable version when publishing is not authorized. The page answers its main question and sources support its claims. Measure relevant queries, leads or revenue over a stated period. Producing thin pages that only swap keywords. Assuming fluent AI copy has been fact-checked.","url":"playbooks/content-seo.html"},{"id":"design-system","title":"Design systems: put the brand into practice","summary":"Reuse components, rules and assets across the product.","category":"design","categoryTitle":"Brand and experience","keywords":["design system","tokens","components","typography","logo"],"body":"A design system gives the team shared colors, type, components and interaction rules so pages feel consistent. Use this when the site grows, more people contribute or similar components keep being rebuilt. Shared rules reduce repeated design and development. Complete component states let agents extend pages while preserving usability and brand consistency. Tokens define color, type, spacing and motion. Components define usage, states and behavior. Brand assets need versions, licenses, formats and usage rules. Implement the rules in code and test them with real content. Approved brand direction, logo, fonts and asset licenses Existing libraries, page types, content and language needs Audit repeated styles and create semantic tokens and a component inventory. Define interaction and responsive behavior for buttons, forms, navigation and content modules. Deliver working components, example pages and a version history from a shared source. Long and short content, narrow screens and error states work. Design and code share token values; assets have valid usage rights. Inventing new type scales and buttons for every page. Delivering assets without checking their licenses.","url":"playbooks/design-system.html"},{"id":"development-quality","title":"Development delivery and quality","summary":"Deliver working software that others can test and maintain.","category":"build","categoryTitle":"Web and software","keywords":["development","QA","Git","testing","performance","accessibility","CI"],"body":"Development is complete when users can finish their main tasks, understand failures and get help, and another maintainer can take over. Use this for website acceptance, code handoff or recurring defects. Early workflow and integration checks reduce failures after launch. Reproducible builds and handoffs let someone besides the original developer maintain the product. Acceptance covers main paths, errors, permissions, compatibility, SEO and performance. Use real-user data for Core Web Vitals. Lab scores help diagnose issues but do not establish field performance. Separate development, staging and production. Keep secrets out of frontend code, repositories and test records. Approved requirements, prototypes, content, supported devices and browsers Repository, API contracts, test environment and acceptance criteria Read project rules and inspect the current implementation. Make the smallest suitable change. Run checks appropriate to the risk. Verify data, permissions and main user paths. Check mobile, keyboard use, performance and indexing; fix related failures. Deliver a buildable version, running instructions, test evidence and known limits. A clean environment can build the project; main paths have observed results. Changes are reviewable and recoverable. List checks that were not run. Treating a passing build as functional verification. Mocking every dependency and claiming the integration works.","url":"playbooks/development-quality.html"},{"id":"ecommerce-growth","title":"Ecommerce growth: product pages and retention","summary":"Find where buyers drop out before choosing what to change.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["D2C","ecommerce growth","PDP","AOV","LTV","CVR","retention"],"body":"Ecommerce growth means finding losses between arrival and repeat purchase, then improving the stage with the greatest impact. Use this when traffic fails to convert, campaigns weaken or repeat sales need work. Fix purchase barriers before paying for more traffic. Margin, refunds and repeat purchases give a clearer view of growth than revenue alone. Check the view-to-cart-to-checkout-to-purchase funnel by source, device and market. Validate tracking first. Product pages must explain specifications, fit, delivery, returns and trust. Claims and reviews must be real. Consider margin, stock, service cost and opt-outs when planning discounts, subscriptions and retention. Funnel, product and margin data; support and return reasons Campaign rules, stock, audience consent and brand assets Inspect funnel losses and valuable products. Rank hypotheses by impact and evidence. Draft changes to product pages, offers or retention flows. Fix functional and data issues first. Define the main metric, guardrails, observation period and sample interpretation. Purchase and refund paths work; events and revenue reconcile reasonably. Conclusions state sample limits and distinguish seasonal or promotional effects from causation. Watching ROAS while ignoring margin, refunds and new customers. Declaring a winner before enough data is available.","url":"playbooks/ecommerce-growth.html"},{"id":"ecommerce-launch","title":"Ecommerce launch checklist","summary":"Verify transactions, search, measurement and operations before opening the store.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["Shopify","ecommerce launch","checklist","Sally","GA4","Meta","Google Ads"],"body":"An ecommerce launch spans correct transactions, discoverability, measurement, fulfillment and after-sales responsibility. Use this for a new store, migration cutover or a shared acceptance standard. A working checkout can still lose revenue through broken tracking, delivery or notifications. A shared baseline saves each project from rebuilding its launch checklist. Transactions: products, stock, successful and failed payments, refunds, discounts, shipping, tax, policies and order notifications. Search: old URLs and redirects, canonical URLs, titles, descriptions, Open Graph, structured data, robots, sitemap and GSC. Measurement: GA4 funnel, Google Ads conversions, Meta Pixel/CAPI, feeds, consent and deduplication. Operations: email lists and flows, support and warranty routes, DNS/TLS, performance, accessibility, alerts and recovery. Mark each item complete, blocked or not applicable with a reason. Plan unused ad channels without spending automatically. Production and test domains, version, store connections and authorization Markets, transaction rules, channel plans, test orders and launch decision owner Create a delivery matrix with applicability, owner, status, evidence, gaps and next steps. Reuse integrations. Assign event responsibility across the custom storefront, Shopify checkout and server. Test products and addresses with accepted and rejected consent, payment success and failure, refunds and retries. Reconcile orders and events. Switch over within existing launch authorization. Check the domain, redirects, indexing, notifications and monitoring. Each area has evidence; blockers have an explicit go/no-go decision. Each eligible real order maps to one purchase event. Isolate test data, notifications and ad audiences. Equating installed apps or a polished storefront with readiness. Installing Klaviyo, GTM or paid apps without checking their purpose and existing systems.","url":"playbooks/ecommerce-launch.html"},{"id":"fde","title":"FDE: from the client’s workflow to production","summary":"Embed the delivery team in real work, from scoping and integration to operational ownership.","category":"ai","categoryTitle":"AI consulting and FDE","keywords":["FDE","Forward Deployed Engineering","Forward Deployed Engineer","embedded delivery","AI consulting"],"body":"FDE brings an engineering team into the client’s actual workflow. The team stays involved through problem definition, integration and production operation. Use this when delivery requires business judgment, existing systems and real users. Working with real users, data and exceptions reduces the gap between plans and daily use. One team connects scope, acceptance and operations so a pilot can become a dependable system. Forward Deployed Engineering is an engineering delivery approach embedded in the client’s workflow. FDE can also mean an engineer doing this work. Start with one workflow that has an owner, accessible data and a measurable baseline. Agree on the first scope and acceptance criteria. Review working iterations with users and test main paths and exceptions. Define monitoring, incident handling and knowledge transfer before launch. Users, current workflow, data/access, failure samples and baseline Initial scope, budget, acceptance owner and ongoing operating responsibilities Map the work with the people doing it. Confirm problems, data, exceptions and decisions. Choose one use case, agree on acceptance and assess benefit, integration cost and risk. Build a working version, connect agreed systems, test with users and record decisions. Confirm release, monitoring, recovery, support and knowledge transfer. Expand after the first workflow meets acceptance. The working system passes agreed samples and criteria, with results compared to the baseline. Launch and operations have owners, monitoring and handoff paths; unfinished items remain traceable. Stopping at a demo without testing real data, access and exceptions. Treating FDE as an AI employee or a staffing-replacement guarantee.","url":"playbooks/fde.html"},{"id":"ga4-commerce","title":"GA4 ecommerce events and conversion rates","summary":"Connect product views, carts, checkouts and purchases into a funnel you can reconcile.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["GA4","conversion rate","CVR","purchase","refund","event","DebugView","funnel"],"body":"GA4 records customer actions as events. Connecting views, carts, checkouts and purchases reveals the shopping funnel. Use this to inspect drop-offs, calculate conversion rates or verify purchase tracking. Funnel losses show whether to improve the product page, cart or checkout. Inconsistent denominators, amounts and events lead to conflicting conclusions. Core events include view_item_list/select_item, view_item, add_to_cart, begin_checkout, purchase and refund. Add shipping and payment events as needed. Define triggers, item_id, items, value and currency. Use a stable transaction_id to match purchases and refunds to orders. Purchase session conversion rate is sessions with a purchase divided by all sessions in the same scope. Orders per session and all-key-event rate differ. If 100 of 10,000 sessions include a purchase, the rate is 1%. Align time zone, device, market, period and consent coverage. GA4, Shopify and Ads have different attribution and tracking limits. Order accounting is the transaction baseline; reports need not match exactly. Property, data stream, event sources and test environment Product/variant ID mappings, tax and shipping value rules, de-identified test orders Define one sender per event and parameter. Check native integrations and gaps between custom storefront and hosted checkout. Verify cross-domain source continuity; avoid self or payment-provider referrals. Do not send personal data such as email to analytics. Check event order, values and currencies in DebugView, requests and reports. Test reloads, failed payments, refunds and consent. Deliver a funnel and metric dictionary with numerators, denominators, test exclusions and reasons for discrepancies. Reloads do not duplicate purchases; refunds match the original transaction. Reports show funnels by channel/device and reproduce the agreed conversion rate. Sending purchase on a checkout-button click or failed payment. Calling orders divided by users a session conversion rate.","url":"playbooks/ga4-commerce.html"},{"id":"geo","title":"GEO: help AI search understand your content","summary":"Maintain verifiable answers and first-party evidence, then observe citations.","category":"search","categoryTitle":"SEO and GEO","keywords":["GEO","AEO","AI search","ChatGPT","Perplexity","AI Overviews","llms.txt"],"body":"GEO helps AI search find, understand and cite a brand’s content through clear answers, verifiable facts and updates. Use this for AI-search visibility or organizing product and expert knowledge. Buyers discover brands through AI answers; usable sources help systems describe them accurately. Maintained facts support consistency across the website, sales and conventional search. Google AI search uses SEO fundamentals and requires no special AI schema or llms.txt. Check other platforms separately. Describe entities, products, relationships, limitations and dates clearly. Structured data must match visible content. A citation is an observation for a platform, prompt and time. It is not a stable ranking or a guarantee of inclusion. Buyer questions, target platforms and markets First-party product facts, cases, sources and indexable pages Map questions to evidence and repair crawling or content gaps first. Write direct answers with verifiable support, consistent entity details and relevant internal links. Use fixed questions to observe answers. Record platform, prompt, date, cited URLs and limits. Claims trace to sources and target platforms can access the pages. Reports separate content changes, citation observations and unproven causal claims. Guaranteeing a citation in ChatGPT or Google. Using llms.txt as a substitute for SEO or access control.","url":"playbooks/geo.html"},{"id":"google-commerce","title":"Google Ads and product feeds","summary":"Connect conversions, product data and landing pages before spending.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["Google Ads","Merchant Center","Google & YouTube","Shopping","PMax","Performance Max","feed","GTIN","enhanced conversions","catalog"],"body":"Google Ads runs campaigns; Merchant Center supplies product data. Accurate products and purchase reports provide the foundation. Use this for Shopping setup, disapproved products or duplicate conversions. Missing conversions weaken bidding signals; incorrect feeds can cause disapproval or price mismatches. Separate integration acceptance from campaign activation so spending has measurable outcomes. Ads handles campaigns and conversions; Merchant Center handles products and eligibility; GA4 analyzes behavior; GSC covers search indexing. Avoid using both native Ads and imported GA4 purchases as primary bidding conversions for the same purchase. Check how observation actions enter campaign goals. Keep stable IDs, applicable brand/GTIN identifiers, price, currency, stock, images, live URLs and shipping/return policies consistent. Enhanced conversions use approved first-party data for matching. Hashing does not remove consent or data responsibilities. Consider Shopify’s official Google & YouTube integration first. Test headless checkout, domains and product links end to end. Existing Ads, Merchant Center and GA4 accounts; target markets Product identifiers, shipping/return policies, conversion definitions and authorization Inspect links and conversion actions. Define one primary purchase source with value, currency and transaction_id. Sync products through supported integrations/APIs and inspect diagnostics. Do not invent GTINs or submit development URLs. Test order reconciliation, reload deduplication, cross-domain attribution and consent. Verify current enhanced-conversion support. Deliver eligibility and disapproval findings with fixes. Activate budgets, assets and campaigns only within advertising authorization. Sample prices, stock and URLs match landing pages; disapprovals have a reason and action. One purchase creates the intended bidding signal. Integration completion does not establish campaign results. Counting one order in multiple primary conversion actions. Pointing feeds to a Shopify theme when customers should use the custom storefront.","url":"playbooks/google-commerce.html"},{"id":"headless-commerce","title":"Headless commerce: integration boundaries","summary":"Verify the custom storefront, Shopify checkout and external tools separately.","category":"build","categoryTitle":"Web and software","keywords":["headless","Astro","Sanity","Shopify","Storefront API","XTERRA","Sally","web pixel","301","consent"],"body":"Headless commerce separates the storefront from transactions. The frontend handles browsing; Shopify handles products, cart and checkout. Use this for a custom frontend such as Astro or an app that does not appear after installation. A flexible storefront may need its own app, tracking and consent integrations. Clear data and route ownership prevents configuration in the wrong system. The XTERRA snapshot uses Astro for the storefront, Sanity for marketing content, and Shopify for products, cart and hosted checkout. Verify checkout/order integrations, custom browsing/form events and email flows separately. Implement SEO and redirects at the domain and routing layer receiving old URLs. Shopify redirect imports alone may not affect Astro. Custom consent choices may not reach Shopify, analytics or marketing. Test cross-domain behavior, withdrawal and rejection. Shopify Dev MCP provides documentation and validation; it does not grant store read/write access. Check each account and API scope. Repository/commit, storefront/checkout domains and deployment environments Data sources, event and integration owners, app support and secure connections Map data, events, domains and owners. Check each app’s headless APIs, webhooks and limits. Test product updates, cart/discounts, checkout return, order events, form-to-CRM and retries separately. Test old URLs at the real routing layer. Verify production canonicals, indexing controls and feed URLs. Resolve the XTERRA README/Klaviyo-document discrepancy using code and account evidence before changing anything. Do not reinstall integrations or activate drafts by assumption. Data and events are traceable without duplicates; unknown hosted settings are explicit gaps. Handoff covers domains, ownership and failures. A successful Git push does not prove customer availability. Applying theme instructions to a custom frontend. Treating repository descriptions as proof of live flows or orders.","url":"playbooks/headless-commerce.html"},{"id":"hosting-architecture","title":"Hosting, DNS, CDN and environments","summary":"Know who runs each service and how domains, applications, data and email connect.","category":"operations","categoryTitle":"Hosting and operations","keywords":["hosting","AWS","GCP","Azure","VPS","DNS","CDN","SSL","TLS"],"body":"A website depends on its domain, DNS, hosting, content and network services. Knowing the layers helps with selection and troubleshooting. Use this for hosting plans, domain changes or website/email connection problems. Hosting choices affect availability, cost, maintenance and recovery. Understanding DNS dependencies prevents a website move from breaking email. Managed Shopify/Webflow hosting and self-managed VPS/cloud infrastructure have different responsibilities. Use the actual contract. Separate registration, DNS, CDN, TLS, application and database layers when diagnosing problems. Plan development, staging, production, data region, RTO/RPO, backups and monitoring. State assumptions in traffic and cost estimates. Architecture, traffic, data types and current DNS zone Account owners, budget, SLA, backup and recovery needs Map architecture, DNS and dependencies; assign platform, Tenten and client responsibilities. Compare managed and self-managed options, costs and scaling. Inspect CDN, TLS and persistence. Deliver environment configuration, backup/recovery, monitoring and access handoff. Production DNS and TLS work; email MX/TXT records remain intact. Restarting or rebuilding preserves persistent data; isolated restore evidence exists. Treating DNS TTL as a guarantee of an instant switch. Backing up code without the database and media.","url":"playbooks/hosting-architecture.html"},{"id":"klaviyo","title":"Klaviyo: newsletters and automated journeys","summary":"Use subscription and shopping behavior for welcome emails, checkout reminders and product guidance.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["Klaviyo","Sherry","EDM","newsletter","welcome","abandoned cart","flow","winback","email automation"],"body":"Klaviyo automates ecommerce email and SMS based on subscriptions and shopping activity. Use this for welcome emails, checkout reminders, product guidance or more relevant newsletters. Behavior-based flows send relevant messages without rebuilding every campaign. Exit rules stop abandoned-checkout reminders after purchase and marketing after unsubscribe. Klaviyo handles email, SMS and journeys. Profiles represent people, lists hold members, segments use dynamic rules, flows react to triggers and campaigns are one-off sends. An email address or profile is not marketing consent. Email and SMS consent are separate; order and service messages serve different purposes. Start with welcome, checkout abandonment and post-purchase guidance. Consider browse reminders and winback later. Durable goods often need care advice and accessories. Separate native Checkout Started/Placed Order events from custom form events. Placed Order does not prove payment; check payment, cancellation and refund states. Do not assume two-way unsubscribe sync. Official guidance says a Shopify unsubscribe does not automatically unsubscribe the person in Klaviyo; handle suppression explicitly. Existing sender and Shopify integration; list sources, consent and unsubscribe records Sending domain, assets, flow goals and internal test recipients Inventory platforms and live/draft flows. Map native order and custom form data to prevent duplicate integrations or sends. Define field/event mappings, sender authentication, segments, triggers, waits, exclusions and exits. Keep new flows in Draft. Test consent, purchase exits, unsubscribe suppression and retry deduplication. Isolate historical imports from welcome triggers. Use supported APIs/integrations. Deliver test-email receipts, drafts and capability gaps; send only within task authorization. Profiles, events and subscription states are independently verified; test messages and links are correct. Purchases stop the relevant reminders; unsubscribes stop marketing. Document sync gaps. Assess clicks, valid orders and unsubscribes. Attributed revenue does not establish incremental revenue. Running Shopify and Klaviyo abandonment flows at once. Treating all historical customers as subscribed or using opens as the only metric.","url":"playbooks/klaviyo.html"},{"id":"launch","title":"Website launch: readiness and cutover","summary":"Check SEO, transactions, tracking, DNS and recovery before launch.","category":"operations","categoryTitle":"Hosting and operations","keywords":["go live","launch","checklist","DNS","GA4","GSC","301"],"body":"Launch moves an accepted version into production and verifies continuity for users, search engines and operations. Use this for a new site, redesign cutover or launch decision. A launch is a system change; one missing dependency can interrupt traffic, transactions or data. Clear go/no-go and recovery conditions help the team decide quickly. Cover functionality, content, SEO, measurement, privacy, DNS/TLS, performance, backups and ownership. Inventory existing GA4/GSC first. Activate Ads/Meta for planned channels; check titles, descriptions and Open Graph separately. Production needs its own checks for indexing, forms, payments and tracking even if preview worked. Approved version, domain, release window, decision owner and operations contacts Acceptance, redirects, events, DNS, backup and recovery plans Build the go/no-go checklist; verify staging paths, SEO, tracking and required policies. Inspect production DNS/TLS/email, sync, environment variables and monitoring; prepare a recoverable version. Cut over within existing authorization. Remove production noindex or password restrictions only where appropriate. Check representative URLs, orders/forms, events and GSC. Deliver a dated, versioned release record and monitoring plan. Production tasks work, old URLs map correctly and analytics events deduplicate. No unintended staging restrictions remain; ownership is handed over and failed items have decisions. Removing protection from admin or preview content with the public site. Testing only the homepage.","url":"playbooks/launch.html"},{"id":"lifecycle-crm","title":"CRM, email and customer journeys","summary":"Connect leads, purchases and follow-up so handoffs do not lose work.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["CRM","HubSpot","email","Klaviyo","automation","lead","retention","EDM"],"body":"A CRM records customer and opportunity status. A customer journey defines when to provide information or service. Use this for incoming leads, scattered lists or follow-up email planning. A form must route the lead to an owner and track the result. Consent, segmentation and exit rules keep communication relevant. Define contacts, companies, deals, sources and lifecycle stages; assign field ownership and deduplication keys. Separate order messages from marketing. Define cross-system ownership for consent, unsubscribes, bounces and deletion; do not assume two-way sync. Verify sending domains and SPF/DKIM/DMARC. Preserve existing mail services when changing DNS. Contact fields, sources, consent records, CRM and sending platform Journey, owners, response times, messages and exit rules Define field mappings, segments, assignment and deduplication. Draft welcome, inquiry, abandonment or retention flows and messages. Use test contacts to verify triggers, exclusions, unsubscribes, deduplication and CRM updates. Each valid form reaches the right system and owner; retries create no duplicate records. Unsubscribed contacts leave inapplicable marketing flows; tests do not reach customers. Treating all historical customers as consenting to marketing. Triggering bulk email during imports.","url":"playbooks/lifecycle-crm.html"},{"id":"measurement","title":"Launch measurement: GA4, GSC, Google Ads and Meta","summary":"Decide what to measure, then connect traffic, behavior and transaction data.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["GA4","Google Search Console","GSC","GTM","Google Ads","Meta Pixel","CAPI","meta tag","tracking","consent"],"body":"Measurement turns website behavior into evidence. GA4, GSC and ad platforms answer different questions. Use this at launch, before campaigns or to investigate traffic and conversion. Reliable measurement shows whether traffic and campaigns produce results. Duplicate purchases, wrong currencies and consent errors distort bidding and decisions. GA4 covers behavior, GSC covers Google search and indexing, and Ads/Meta cover campaigns and conversions. Attribution differs. SEO titles/descriptions, Open Graph tags and Meta Pixel serve different purposes. GTM is an optional deployment tool. Define ecommerce triggers, transaction_id, items, value and currency. Do not send personal data indiscriminately to analytics. Existing analytics, search, tag-manager and ad-account IDs with authorized connections Events, purchase definition, data sources, test orders and market privacy requirements Inventory native apps, theme code, GTM and server implementations; assign event ownership. Include GA4, GSC, Ads and Meta in the baseline review. Connect planned channels and document why others do not apply. Test consent acceptance/rejection, purchase, refund and reloads. Match Meta event_name and event_id across Pixel/CAPI. Compare DebugView/platform test events to orders. Keep payload, timing and deduplication evidence; explain attribution differences. One test order creates one valid purchase with matching value and currency. Consent behavior follows approved rules; reports expose no personal data. Checking only that a tag loads. Creating a new property and breaking historical continuity.","url":"playbooks/measurement.html"},{"id":"meta-tracking","title":"Meta Pixel, CAPI and deduplication","summary":"Report real purchases without counting browser and server copies twice.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["Meta","Facebook","Instagram","Pixel","CAPI","Conversions API","event_id","eventID","ad tracking"],"body":"Meta tracking reports purchases to Facebook and Instagram advertising. Pixel sends from the browser; CAPI sends from the server. Use this before Meta campaigns or when purchase totals look wrong. Valid purchase events support ad learning and evaluation. Browser and server tracking complement each other, but duplicate purchases inflate ROAS. Pixel sends browser events; CAPI sends server events. Neither is an SEO meta or Open Graph tag. The core path includes ViewContent, AddToCart, InitiateCheckout and Purchase. Keep value, currency and product IDs consistent. For the same event, match event_name and use the browser eventID as the server event_id. Reuse a stable ID for that action. Shopify’s official Meta channel covers some events. Verify custom headless storefront coverage separately. CAPI still requires appropriate consent. Sharing levels, customer data and purposes must follow approved rules. Existing business/ad accounts, Pixel/dataset, catalog and access Event ownership, product ID mappings, consent rules and test orders Inspect official channels, GTM, code and server senders. Assign clear event ownership. Configure supported APIs/native integrations within authorization. Match content_ids to the catalog. Use Test Events and diagnostics for purchases, rejected consent, reloads and retries. Inspect browser/server pairing. Deliver de-identified payloads, timestamps, matched IDs and order-value reconciliation; list uncovered paths. One test purchase becomes one valid conversion, with evidence of browser/server pairing. Rejected sharing follows approved rules; events reveal no extra customer data. Duplicating sends from the official app and manual Pixel. Generating a new event_id on each retry or relying only on Pixel Helper.","url":"playbooks/meta-tracking.html"},{"id":"migration","title":"Magento to Shopify migration","summary":"Preserve products, transactions, search and measurement through a tested cutover.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["Magento","Shopify","migration","Exxterra","XTERRA","301","redirect"],"body":"Migration moves products, content, customer relationships and search entry points to a new system while preserving the jobs users need to do. Use this for Magento-to-Shopify or platform/URL-structure changes. URLs, search traffic and transaction records are assets; omissions interrupt revenue and operations. Rehearsal and incremental sync expose risks while recovery remains manageable. Inventory URLs, traffic pages, products/variants, customers, orders, media, discounts and integrations. Permanently redirect each old URL to its closest relevant page; return 404/410 when no replacement exists. For headless, test at the actual receiving route. Do not assume passwords or logins transfer. Verify historical orders, gift cards and notification side effects separately. Old and target URLs, exports, traffic and URL inventory Included/excluded data, SKU/ID maps and system write ownership Cutover window, incremental data, decision owner and recovery conditions Create data mappings, URL/redirect lists, functional gaps and integration inventory. Dry-run in a test environment. Reconcile SKUs, currencies, counts, relationships and media; isolate notification and tracking side effects. Plan freezes/incremental sync, DNS, GSC, event deduplication and cutover. Execute within authorization. Record last sync, cutover checks and recovery boundaries; monitor afterward. High-value old URLs reach relevant indexable pages in one hop, without loops or chains. Product/order samples, shopping paths, analytics/ad events and consent have test evidence. Recovery after new orders includes reconciliation, not just a DNS reversal. Changing positioning, URLs and platform together without a baseline. Moving products while forgetting GSC, tracking, support or account activation.","url":"playbooks/migration.html"},{"id":"operations-security","title":"Operations, security and recovery","summary":"Detect failures, prove backups can restore and assign incident decisions.","category":"operations","categoryTitle":"Hosting and operations","keywords":["operations","security","backup","restore","incident","RTO","RPO","permissions","monitoring"],"body":"Operations keeps services available. Incident preparation gives the team a way to detect, handle and recover from failures. Use this for hosting handoff, backups or recovery that currently depends on one person. A backup needs a tested recovery process. Least privilege and separate environments limit the impact of mistakes and exposure. RTO is the acceptable recovery time; RPO is the acceptable data-loss window. Set both from business needs. Use secure connections for accounts and secrets. Review and revoke staff, vendor and agent access. Monitor availability, critical tasks, errors and backups. Check update compatibility and retain a recovery path. Systems, permissions, backup locations and recovery requirements Incident owners, contacts, change windows and contractual responsibilities Check least privilege, retention and update procedures; record fixable risks. Restore in isolation and measure recovery time and missing data. Set monitoring thresholds, recipients and escalation owners. Test actual alert delivery. Stabilize service and preserve evidence during incidents. Record the timeline, impact, repair and follow-up. Actual restore results exist; alerts reach the right on-call role. Incidents and changes have versions, owners and traceable records without exposed secrets. Promising uncontracted 24/7 support or response SLAs. Deleting incident records or restoring old data without reconciliation.","url":"playbooks/operations-security.html"},{"id":"paid-media","title":"Google Ads, Meta and campaign delivery","summary":"Connect goals, measurement and landing pages before spending.","category":"growth","categoryTitle":"Marketing and data","keywords":["Google Ads","Meta","paid media","ROAS","CAC","UTM"],"body":"Paid ads bring an audience to a page that can produce an inquiry or purchase. Judge them by business results and cost. Use this to plan campaigns, adjust budgets or decide whether to continue. Advertising cannot repair misleading product information or broken purchases. Budgets and guardrail metrics keep experiments bounded. Choose the main conversion from business goals. Separate primary and observation events to avoid counting the same outcome twice. Keep audience, message, creative and landing page consistent. Use naming and UTMs for channel comparison. Read ROAS and CAC with margin, refunds, attribution and new-customer share. Platform attribution does not establish incremental contribution. Goals, approved budget and period, product/service margin Accounts, conversions, audience consent, assets and landing pages Inspect accounts, tracking, search terms/audiences, policies and conversion quality. Draft campaign structure, creative, UTMs, exclusions and tests. Verify pages, events, amounts and budget. Publish within existing authorization or deliver drafts. Set review intervals and stop conditions; avoid large changes based on a single day. The main conversion has a clear counting source and the landing page task works. Reports include spend, valid outcomes, sample limits and the next testable hypothesis. Activating unapproved spend or counting tracking tests as real sales. Adding orders attributed by different platforms together.","url":"playbooks/paid-media.html"},{"id":"platform-choice","title":"Choosing Webflow, Shopify, WordPress or headless","summary":"Choose for content, transactions and operations, including long-term cost.","category":"build","categoryTitle":"Web and software","keywords":["Webflow","Shopify","WordPress","Next.js","headless","platform","architecture"],"body":"Platform choice balances content management, commerce, flexibility and maintenance against the work the website must do. Use this for a new site, platform limits or a custom-storefront decision. The platform shapes editing efficiency, integration cost and maintenance. Test difficult requirements early to uncover plan limitations. Consider Webflow for content marketing, Shopify for commerce and WordPress for existing ecosystem or plugin needs. Multichannel content, complex integrations or permissions may justify headless/custom development, with more operational responsibility. Compare content models, languages, SEO, integrations, portability and total cost. Verify capabilities against the current plan. Editing and transaction workflows, markets and permissions Existing data, integrations, hosting, budget and maintenance capacity Build a requirements-by-platform matrix with limits, dependencies and exit options. Run a small proof of concept for risky payments, search, preview or integrations. Deliver the architecture decision, cost assumptions and handoff responsibilities. Recommendations trace to requirements; critical features have current documentation or proof-of-concept evidence. Costs include dates, usage and plan assumptions. Do not purchase without authorization. Choosing a familiar tool without checking operations. Assuming headless is always faster or cheaper.","url":"playbooks/platform-choice.html"},{"id":"project-brief","title":"Project kickoff and digital consulting","summary":"Put the problem, constraints and decisions on one page before building.","category":"start","categoryTitle":"Work and consulting","keywords":["brief","requirements","consulting","discovery","scope","KPI","acceptance"],"body":"A project brief gives everyone the same starting point: the problem, expected result and exclusions. Use this at kickoff, when scope changes or when definitions of done differ. A clear business problem prevents extra features that change no outcomes. Deliverables and acceptance criteria make scope and cost easier to manage. Give goals a baseline, target and observation period. Establish a baseline when data is missing. Separate essentials, deferred work and exclusions. Record dependencies, risks and change decisions. Digital consulting connects people, processes, data and systems. Business goals, audience, current workflow and problems Budget range, timeline, contracts and systems Decision makers, data owners and handoff recipients Read existing documents and data; map the current work and repetition. Compare problems, options, benefit, cost and assumptions. Propose the smallest useful validation. Define scope, responsibilities, milestones, acceptance and a change log. Each essential requirement has an artifact, acceptance owner and observable condition. Uncertainties have a verification path; unresolved dependencies constrain date commitments. Inventing a revenue-improvement percentage. Estimating exploratory work as fixed delivery.","url":"playbooks/project-brief.html"},{"id":"shopify-foundation","title":"Shopify setup: connect the operations","summary":"Define products, orders and market rules before themes and apps.","category":"commerce","categoryTitle":"Shopify and ecommerce","keywords":["Shopify","ecommerce","checkout","products","payments","shipping","tax","Markets"],"body":"Shopify manages commerce operations: products, orders, payments and inventory. Setup must establish how each transaction is handled. Use this for new stores, store handoffs, new markets or logistics integrations. A finished storefront still needs correct payments, fulfillment and refunds. Consistent data definitions reduce inventory, pricing and order errors. SKUs/variants identify sellable units. Assign sources of truth for products, inventory and orders; prevent conflicting ERP/3PL writes. Markets determine language, currency, pricing, availability, tax, payment and shipping. The merchant’s specialists confirm tax and legal rules. Use native features first. Verify checkout, API and Markets capabilities for the current plan before adding apps. Product samples, SKU/inventory sources, historical orders and customer needs Markets, payment/shipping, returns, tax and operating owners Define product fields and a market matrix with field-level sources of truth. Propose the theme, apps and integrations with costs, permissions and removal options. Test payment success/failure, shipping/tax, discounts, stockouts, partial refunds/returns, restocking and notifications. Include excluded delivery addresses for bulky goods. Sample orders match approved amounts, currencies, shipping and tax rules. Inventory, orders and events agree; test data does not enter reported production revenue. Treating successful tax configuration as legal compliance. Adding overlapping apps without data ownership.","url":"playbooks/shopify-foundation.html"},{"id":"technical-seo","title":"Technical SEO and permanent redirects","summary":"Help search engines reach the right pages and preserve existing search entry points.","category":"search","categoryTitle":"SEO and GEO","keywords":["SEO","301 redirect","canonical","sitemap","robots","GSC","hreflang","indexing"],"body":"Technical SEO helps search engines find and index the right pages. Permanent redirects connect old URLs to new locations. Use this for launches, URL changes, traffic drops or missing indexed pages. Unreachable pages or ambiguous URLs limit organic visibility. Migration mapping and monitoring reduce omissions and diagnosis effort; rankings may still fluctuate. Robots rules control crawling. Noindex controls indexing and must be readable by the crawler. Canonical suggests the main version. Sitemaps should contain canonical, indexable URLs. Check hreflang, self-canonicals and reciprocal language links. Use 301/308 for permanent moves with the closest relevant mapping. Google recommends keeping redirects for at least a year, and longer when possible. Production/old URLs, GSC, traffic and internal-link inventories Indexing scope, markets/languages, platform and DNS/CDN limits Crawl status codes, indexing directives, canonicals, internal links, sitemaps and structured data. Map old to new URLs. Verify one-hop permanent redirects, 200 destinations and no loops. Fix internal links, metadata and sitemaps. Submit to the authorized GSC property and inspect sample URLs. Consider Change of Address for domain moves. Monitor indexing, traffic and 404s before and after. Important pages are crawlable and indexable. Staging has access protection beyond robots. Redirects, canonicals, hreflang and sitemaps agree; monitoring has dates. Redirecting every 404 to the homepage, creating soft 404s. Assuming sitemap submission guarantees indexing or ranking.","url":"playbooks/technical-seo.html"},{"id":"tenten-context","title":"What Tenten does","summary":"Understand how branding, websites, marketing and AI fit into a project.","category":"start","categoryTitle":"Work and consulting","keywords":["onboarding","company","services","digital consultant","Tenten"],"body":"Tenten turns business problems into brands, websites, marketing and AI workflows. Work in one area usually affects the others. Use this when joining the team or explaining a project to a client. Clients need a solved problem and accountable delivery; the website is one part. Shared briefs, decisions and acceptance reduce gaps between strategy, design and development. Public services cover branding, design/development, digital transformation, SEO/GEO, Shopify, CMS and hosting. AI work includes workflows and enterprise knowledge systems. Connect audience, content/experience, systems/data and post-launch measurement in each project. This library provides suggested practices. Prices, SLAs and commitments come from the project agreement, not assumptions about the website. Client goals, audience and current problems Service scope, existing systems, delivery and operating owners Map needs to services and outcomes; show data, system and team handoffs. Identify missing business decisions, assumptions, constraints and independent work. Create a one-page brief, deliverable list and acceptance plan, then choose relevant articles. Each deliverable supports a goal and has an owner. Commitments have contractual or client confirmation; unknowns are explicit. Using a tool name as the client’s goal. Presenting general advice as existing policy or a proven client result.","url":"playbooks/tenten-context.html"},{"id":"ux-conversion","title":"UX, information architecture and conversion","summary":"Help people find information, understand choices and finish important tasks.","category":"design","categoryTitle":"Brand and experience","keywords":["UX","UI","CRO","site architecture","forms","accessibility","conversion"],"body":"UX is the experience of completing a task. Information architecture organizes content; conversion design makes the next action clear. Use this when people cannot find information, finish forms or complete purchases. A website that hides answers wastes search and ad traffic. Clear paths, forms and error handling reduce confusion and abandonment. Organize navigation, pages, content and calls to action around user tasks rather than the company org chart. Forms and shopping flows need loading, empty, error, success and recovery states. Keyboard access, focus, labels, contrast and mobile use are basic requirements. Interpret conversion changes with traffic quality. Priority audiences, main tasks, funnel data or support questions Content inventory, device mix and brand rules Map task flows, sitemap and page order; state the concern each step resolves. Build an interactive prototype covering main and failed paths. Test keyboard use, forms, narrow screens and actions. Plan experiments when traffic is sufficient. Main tasks work from entry to completion; errors are understandable and recoverable. Events match the hypothesis; reported improvements include sample and period. Delivering only a desktop homepage. Equating more clicks with better business outcomes.","url":"playbooks/ux-conversion.html"}]};
